{"entries":[{"title":"Untitled","url":"/contact/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Visit & contact Visit & contact Visit the cafe 47 Druid Street, London SE1 2HQ (2-minute walk from London Bridge station, behind the Maltby Street Market arches) Open Mon–Sat 7:30am–4pm. Closed Sundays. Order online Free UK shipping on orders over £25. European shipping available. Browse this week's roasts or email shop@heron.coffee with questions. Wholesale We supply 14 cafes across South London with weekly delivery. Minimum 8kg/week. Email wholesale@heron.coffee for the full menu and pricing. Subscriptions Monthly bag of one of our roasts, shipped same-day we roast. Sign up at subscribe@heron.coffee — choose blend, single-origin, or rotating. Press & general hello@heron.coffee — we answer within two business days.","headings":["Visit & contact","Visit the cafe","Order online","Wholesale","Subscriptions","Press & general"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/cupping-notes-july/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Cupping notes — July Cupping notes — July Cupping notes — July Three quick impressions from this morning's cupping table. The washed Yirgacheffe is showing bergamot and black tea now that it has rested a fortnight off roast. The Guji natural is all blueberry — almost too much at first crack plus twenty seconds, so we are pulling the drop two degrees earlier next batch. And the new Huila lot lands somewhere pleasantly in between: red apple, panela, a long clean finish. All three will be on the brew bar from Saturday.","headings":["Cupping notes — July","Cupping notes — July"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/grinder-buying-guide/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Choosing a grinder for home espresso Choosing a grinder for home espresso The honest answer If you can afford to spend more on the grinder than the espresso machine, do that. A cheap grinder with a great machine will always lose to a great grinder with a cheap machine. We've tested both combinations on the bar. Three we recommend Under £200 — Eureka Mignon Specialita. Stepless, quiet, holds calibration through a busy week. The dial is fiddly but you'll touch it twice and then leave it alone for months. £200–£500 — DF64 Gen 2. Single-dose, low retention, 64mm flat burrs. The cheapest grinder we trust to give us repeatable shots. Comes with a slightly questionable funnel; replace it. £500+ — Niche Zero. The one we use at home. Conical burrs, near-zero retention, ergonomic enough to leave on the counter without flinching every time you look at it. Things to ignore Burr coating type, RPM, motor wattage, \"AI grind suggestions\". Marketing. The variables that matter are consistency (how tight is the particle distribution?) and retention (how much old coffee gets stuck inside between doses?). What we use on the bar Mahlkönig E80 Supreme for espresso, EK43 for filter. Combined retail price exceeds our entire cafe fit-out from year one. They're worth it for a 200-cups-a-day operation; they would be ridiculous in a kitchen.","headings":["Choosing a grinder for home espresso","The honest answer","Three we recommend","Things to ignore","What we use on the bar"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文HeronSingle-origin coffee, roasted on Druid Street every Tuesday morning.Get Started Learn MoreA small London coffee roasteryHeron Coffee is a single-origin roaster on Druid Street, Bermondsey. We roast 12kg batches every Tuesday morning. We ship within 48 hours of the roast date.The cafe is open Monday to Saturday, 7:30am to 4pm. Online orders ship across the UK and Europe. Allow 2-3 working days.This week's roastsFinca La Esperanza (Colombia, washed) — citrus, brown sugar, milk chocolate. Roasted 14 April.Sidamo Guji (Ethiopia, natural) — blueberry, jasmine, dark molasses. Roasted 14 April.Cerrado (Brazil, pulped natural) — hazelnut, dark cocoa, low acidity — our espresso default. Roasted 14 April.Subscribe to a monthly bag and we'll ship the same day we roast.Visit the cafe47 Druid Street, London SE1 2HQ (2-minute walk from London Bridge station, behind the Maltby Street Market)Open Mon–Sat 7:30am–4pm. Closed Sundays.What we're brewing this morningEspresso: Cerrado (default) or Finca La Esperanza (£0.50 supplement)Filter: Sidamo Guji on V60, Finca La Esperanza on ChemexDecaf: Mexican Chiapas (Swiss water process), available all dayRecent journal entriesWhy we roast on Tuesdays — the workflow that lets a small team ship fresh coffee every weekChoosing a grinder for home espresso — the only piece of equipment that matters, and the three we recommendThe Sidamo Guji story — how this season's natural-process Ethiopian made it from Yirgacheffe to BermondseyRead all journal posts · Browse by topicWholesaleWe supply 14 cafes across South London. The minimum order is 8kg per week with weekly delivery. Email wholesale@heron.coffee for pricing and the full list of origins and blends.","headings":["Heron","A small London coffee roastery","This week's roasts","Visit the cafe","What we're brewing this morning","Recent journal entries","Wholesale"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/posts/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Journal Journal Journal Notes from the roastery: workflow, equipment, origins, occasional opinions. Why we roast on Tuesdays — How a small team ships fresh coffee every week, and why we chose Tuesday. Choosing a grinder for home espresso — The one piece of gear that changes what you taste, plus three picks at three price points. The Sidamo Guji story — How this season's natural-process Ethiopian went from Yirgacheffe to Bermondsey. Subscribe to the RSS feed or Atom feed to follow new entries.","headings":["Journal","Journal"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/privacy/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Privacy Privacy Privacy This site does not collect personal data, set cookies, or track visitors.","headings":["Privacy","Privacy"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/sidamo-guji-story/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>The Sidamo Guji story The Sidamo Guji story Where it comes from Sidamo Guji is grown by 240 smallholder farmers in Hambela Wamena, southern Ethiopia. They farm at 1,950 to 2,200 metres. Cherries dry on raised beds for 18-22 days, then get hand-sorted three times before export. Our 60kg sack reached London on 28 March, three months after harvest. It came via Mombasa and Antwerp. The supplier is Sucafina Specialty, who pay the co-op 30% above the local C-market price. What it tastes like Cup notes: blueberry, jasmine, dark molasses. Best brewed as a V60 or Chemex. We pulled it as espresso this week and it was clean — bright but not thin. Brew recipe (V60, single cup): 15g coffee, ground for filter (around 600μm) 250g water at 94°C 45-second bloom with 50g Pour to 150g over 15 seconds, then to 250g over 30 seconds Total contact time around 2:45 Why it costs what it costs This roast is £14 for 250g. That is in the upper third of our menu. The price covers the small lot size, the high-altitude premium, and fast shipping from origin. This coffee was on the tree at 2,000m just a hundred days ago. We sell about half our Sidamo to other London roasters. If you run a cafe and want it on bar, email wholesale@heron.coffee.","headings":["The Sidamo Guji story","Where it comes from","What it tastes like","Why it costs what it costs"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/tags/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Tags Tags Browse by tag Browse all content organised by topic. Select a tag to discover related pages. Featured Tags (29) Equipment (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Choosing a grinder for home espresso - The only piece of equipment that materially affects what you taste, and the three we recommend at three price points. Espresso (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Choosing a grinder for home espresso - The only piece of equipment that materially affects what you taste, and the three we recommend at three price points. Ethiopia (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: The Sidamo Guji story - How this season's natural-process Ethiopian made it from a 240-farmer collective in Yirgacheffe to a small roastery in Bermondsey. Example (7 Posts) April 16, 2026: Page Not Found - Page Not Found for Heron Coffee April 16, 2026: Visit & contact - Visit Heron Coffee on Druid Street, Bermondsey, or order online for UK and European delivery. April 16, 2026: Heron Coffee - Heron Coffee — single-origin roaster on Druid Street, Bermondsey. April 16, 2026: Offline - Offline for Heron Coffee April 16, 2026: Journal - Heron Coffee journal — workflow, equipment, origins, occasional opinions. April 16, 2026: Privacy - Privacy for Heron Coffee April 16, 2026: Tags - Tags for Heron Coffee Origin (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: The Sidamo Guji story - How this season's natural-process Ethiopian made it from a 240-farmer collective in Yirgacheffe to a small roastery in Bermondsey. Quickstart (7 Posts) April 16, 2026: Page Not Found - Page Not Found for Heron Coffee April 16, 2026: Visit & contact - Visit Heron Coffee on Druid Street, Bermondsey, or order online for UK and European delivery. April 16, 2026: Heron Coffee - Heron Coffee — single-origin roaster on Druid Street, Bermondsey. April 16, 2026: Offline - Offline for Heron Coffee April 16, 2026: Journal - Heron Coffee journal — workflow, equipment, origins, occasional opinions. April 16, 2026: Privacy - Privacy for Heron Coffee April 16, 2026: Tags - Tags for Heron Coffee Roasting (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Why we roast on Tuesdays - The workflow that lets a small team ship fresh coffee every week — and the practical reasons we picked Tuesday. Sidamo (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: The Sidamo Guji story - How this season's natural-process Ethiopian made it from a 240-farmer collective in Yirgacheffe to a small roastery in Bermondsey. Sourcing (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: The Sidamo Guji story - How this season's natural-process Ethiopian made it from a 240-farmer collective in Yirgacheffe to a small roastery in Bermondsey. Ssg (7 Posts) April 16, 2026: Page Not Found - Page Not Found for Heron Coffee April 16, 2026: Visit & contact - Visit Heron Coffee on Druid Street, Bermondsey, or order online for UK and European delivery. April 16, 2026: Heron Coffee - Heron Coffee — single-origin roaster on Druid Street, Bermondsey. April 16, 2026: Offline - Offline for Heron Coffee April 16, 2026: Journal - Heron Coffee journal — workflow, equipment, origins, occasional opinions. April 16, 2026: Privacy - Privacy for Heron Coffee April 16, 2026: Tags - Tags for Heron Coffee Workflow (1 Posts) April 16, 2026: Why we roast on Tuesdays - The workflow that lets a small team ship fresh coffee every week — and the practical reasons we picked Tuesday.","headings":["Tags","Browse by tag","Featured Tags (29)","Equipment (1 Posts)","Espresso (1 Posts)","Ethiopia (1 Posts)","Example (7 Posts)","Origin (1 Posts)","Quickstart (7 Posts)","Roasting (1 Posts)","Sidamo (1 Posts)","Sourcing (1 Posts)","Ssg (7 Posts)","Workflow (1 Posts)"]},{"title":"Untitled","url":"/why-we-roast-tuesdays/index.html","content":"Skip to main content Skip to content FeaturesPostsExplore by tagContact Us 🇬🇧 English 🇫🇷 Français 🇸🇦 العربية 🇧🇩 বাংলা 🇨🇿 Čeština 🇩🇪 Deutsch 🇪🇸 Español 🇳🇬 Hausa 🇮🇱 עברית 🇮🇳 हिन्दी 🇮🇩 Indonesia 🇮🇹 Italiano 🇯🇵 日本語 🇰🇷 한국어 🇳🇱 Nederlands 🇵🇱 Polski 🇵🇹 Português 🇷🇴 Română 🇷🇺 Русский 🇸🇪 Svenska 🇹🇭 ไทย 🇵🇭 Filipino 🇹🇷 Türkçe 🇺🇦 Українська 🇻🇳 Tiếng Việt 🇳🇬 Yorùbá 🇨🇳 简体中文 🇹🇼 繁體中文 Home>Why we roast on Tuesdays Why we roast on Tuesdays The short answer Tuesday roasts mean Wednesday post pickup, which means most UK customers receive their coffee on Thursday or Friday — the same week it was roasted. The longer answer Coffee tastes best between 5 and 21 days off-roast. Roast Monday and the post-room queue means it's already day-2 before it ships. Roast Wednesday and the orders that came in over the weekend are now four days stale on our shelves. Tuesday hits the sweet spot. We also use Mondays for green-bean cupping and equipment maintenance, which means the roast on Tuesday morning is always going onto a clean machine with calibrated profiles. What this means for subscribers If you're on the monthly subscription, your coffee leaves us on the Wednesday closest to your usual delivery date. That's why subscriptions don't have a \"next-day\" option — we'd rather wait three days to ship fresh than rush something stale to your door.","headings":["Why we roast on Tuesdays","The short answer","The longer answer","What this means for subscribers"]}]}
